From: ITSMEFRTRUST <215584995+ITSMEFRTRUST@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:26:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 040/138] PCI: apple: Add support for the A8 (T7000)

The iPhone 6 Wi-Fi sits on port 1 of the A8 PCIe controller. Static
analysis of the iOS 12 AppleT7000PCIe kext shows the M1 design minus a
few blocks:

- port clock, refclk, power-down and reset bits live in the common
  ("rc") block, one set of fields per port, instead of the port block;
- PHY 0 is shared by ports 0 and 1 and needs two settings written once;
- the MSI doorbell is hardwired at 0xbffff000 (no MSIADDR register);
- there is no RID-to-SID table, the port's DART has a single stream;
- link status and LINKCMDSTS moved to 0x88 and 0x808.

Add SoC hooks for the controller and port bring-up, skip the MSIADDR
and RID2SID programming when the hardware has none, and give the T7000
its own map_bus so that the unpowered port 0 and downstream buses with
no link are never accessed (iOS panics on such accesses). The PERST#
GPIO is requested asserted and released right before link training, as
iOS does, and an optional pwren-gpios powers the endpoint 100 ms before
the port comes up.

The doorbell Kconfig symbol gets a prompt since the A8 needs a value
other than the default. The MSI domain is torn down when the host bridge
fails to come up, so that the driver can be probed again.

The T7000 bring-up is untested and logs every step. The t7000_stage
module parameter stops it after a given stage. A built-in driver defaults
to doing nothing and a module to doing everything, so the controller is
only touched on an explicit insmod.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
